, $13$ on Pima, and a minority of instances at the next budget), where the weighted regression is ill-conditioned and the spread is numerical, not sampling variance.\npaper/results.tex:10:With $T=R\\|\\bar{\\hat\\phi}-\\phi\\|^2/\\operatorname{tr}\\widehat{\\mathrm{Cov}}(\\hat\\phi)$, about one for an unbiased estimator, the paired Kernel and antithetic permutation estimators and \\texttt{l1\\_reg=False} are unbiased (median $T=0.9$, $0.9$, $1.0$), while \\texttt{num\\_features(10)} is not (median $440$) and \\texttt{auto} is not for some instances (maximum $>2000$); the biased arms are reported separately.\npaper/results.tex:19:The default is not the same as the other \\texttt{shap} settings: \\texttt{num\\_features(10)}, which selects ten features by a lasso path and is biased, has a strict share of $0.68$--$0.93$ at the default budget on the six non-linear $d\\ge16$ cells (Table~\\ref{tab:sens}).\npaper/results.tex:23:At the default budget $2d+2048=2606$ the top-3 strict $P_c$, averaged over instances, is $0.99$ (boosting) and $0.95$ (forest), the tolerance-aware one $1.00$, and the share with strict $P_c\\ge0.9$ is $0.98$ and $0.85$; \\texttt{num\\_features(10)} gives $0.38$ and $0.25$, and at $m=256<d$ \\texttt{l1\\_reg=False} gives $P_c=0$.\npaper/results.tex:29:\\caption{Single-run reliability of the top-3 set against the budget $m$ (\\texttt{shap} Kernel, \\texttt{l1\\_reg=False}; $R=30$--$100$ runs per instance, $40$--$100$ instances). Top: share of instances with $P_c^{\\varepsilon}\\ge0.9$ at $\\varepsilon=0.05$ (filled, band: $95\\%$ instance-bootstrap interval) and with strict $P_c\\ge0.9$ (open, dotted); dashed vertical line: \\texttt{shap} default $2d+2048$. Middle: mean Pearson correlation between a run and the exact $\\phi$. Bottom: share of instances with $P_c\\le x$ at the reference budget printed under each panel. The gap betwee\npaper/results.tex:42:\\caption{Share of instances with strict $P_c\\ge0.9$ (solid, $95\\%$ instance-bootstrap interval) and with $P_c^{\\varepsilon}\\ge0.9$ (dotted) by within-cell quartile of the exact margin $g_3$ (Q1 smallest) at the reference budget of each cell (\\texttt{shap} Kernel, \\texttt{l1\\_reg=False}); $10$ (German Credit), $15$ (ACS Employment) or $25$ instances per quartile. Grey bars: share of the quartile's instances whose tolerance group is flat, where $P_c^{\\varepsilon}$ holds vacuously. The $d\\le10$ panels are at a sampled budget ($127$ or $511$), where the default would enumerate\npaper/results.tex:57:For the paired Kernel and antithetic permutation estimators the log--log slope of the relative $L_2$ error is $-0.49$ to $-0.53$ on every non-linear cell, as sampling theory says; the \\texttt{shap} Kernel explainer falls faster, with slopes of $-0.62$ to $-0.66$ on the two larger datasets and $-0.76$ to $-0.91$ on the $d\\le10$ ones, while with \\texttt{num\\_features(10)} the error hardly falls (slope $-0.02$ to $-0.17$) because the estimator is biased.\npaper/results.tex:70:\\caption{Budget and estimators on German Credit (top) and ACS Employment (bottom), $\\texttt{l1\\_reg=False}$. (a) Median relative $L_2$ error of $\\hat\\phi$ against $m$ for the \\texttt{shap} Kernel explainer (logistic regressio\n[truncated for publication]", "exit": null}}}, {"id": "6dec4717-5098-5365-a16f-88c5723d9c9b", "at": 1795655069.4358175, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 95399, "layer": "worker", "category": "usage", "kind": "session.usage", "role": "experiment-repo", "actor": "experiment-repo", "execution_id": "1f8f1ef86ef8fdfdb9d234dec0344d0a", "trust": "agent-reported", "outcome": null, "label": "usage", "repository": null, "flags": [], "causes": [], "summary": "context usage"}, {"id": "9209b093-ebc4-5758-b4b4-112a96acb439", "at": 1795655069.4427395, "clock": "host", "stream": "runner", "sequence": 95400, "layer": "worker", "category": "message", "kind": "session.session/request_permission", "role": "experiment-repo", "actor": "experiment-repo", "execution_i